    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72789.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 5,
    "turn_text": "Well, we need something to happen right away. Really, the time is running out on this in terms of retaliation and what's coming before the WTO. I tried to get it resolved within the farm bill and both sides were unwilling to really come together and find a compromise then. I wanted to create a certainty a couple years ago, but instead, folks wanted the process to play out. And so this summer, when we lost again before the WTO, Senator Hoeven and I came together and proposed a compromise that could pass the Senate, but the majority of the members of the Senate Agriculture Committee and the majority of members on the floor of the Senate, there was disagreement again, folks wanted to run out the process. So we'll just have to see. I mean, I've been trying to find a way to get this fixed for a long time. The good news is that both the House and the Senate bills that have been proposed would repeal cool. That's very important because there's been a lot of misinformation. Both the House and the Senate bill repeal cool. The only difference is that in the Senate that compromise would make it clear that USDA could develop a product of the U.S. label that had a specific definition and integrity in terms of born, raised, and slaughtered in the U.S. But both proposals, everybody has agreed at this point. We have to file the WTO, we have to repeal COOL, the question is, for those bipartisan strong support for cool, whether or not there is a compromise that would allow a product of the U.S. label that would have some integrity in it.",
