  {
    "episode_id": 72789,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/7/2/72789.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 20,
    "turn_text": "Well, I believe we need to make good nutrition and frankly education in general a top priority for our country We all say children are our future and then where we look at the investments in our country They don't necessarily go to those things that will Keep our children make our children strong and healthy and healthy and well-educated and so on. So I'm all for adding resources if we were able to get the bipartisan support to do it. I'm all for adding resources to support our local schools, the folks with the meals programs who are working very, very hard. And in general, when we look at the multiple ways that schools feel pressure from local property taxes and school funding from states and all the ways that they have pressures on them. I certainly understand that. And I think that the answer is not to say children should be less healthy and more obesity or that children should not have quality schools. I think the answer should be how are we going to step up together and make sure the next generation has what they need to be successful.",
